4	
FPDF Unicode Encoding Crash


FPDFUnicodeEncodingException: Character "‑" at index 29... outside the range supported by "helvetica"

FPDF standard built-in fonts (Helvetica) only support standard latin-1. LLM output inside cost_result["notes"] contained non-breaking hyphens (\u2011), en-dashes (\u2013), or smart quotes, causing pdf.multi_cell() in agent_4_report.py to crash.	Created a centralized _clean() helper function in agent_4_report.py that intercepts all strings and replaces unsupported Unicode dashes, quotes, and symbols with safe ASCII equivalents.	
Before:


python\npdf.multi_cell(value_width, 7, str(value) if str(value).strip() else "-")\n


After:


python\ndef _clean(text: str) -> str:\n replacements = {"\u2011": "-", "\u2013": "-", "\u2018": "'", ...}\n for bad, fix in replacements.items():\n text = str(text).replace(bad, fix)\n return text.encode("latin-1", "replace").decode("latin-1")\n\n_row(pdf, label, value): ... pdf.multi_cell(value_width, 7, _clean(value))\n

5	
PostgreSQL Connection Refused


psycopg2.OperationalError: connection to server at "localhost" (127.0.0.1), port 5433 failed

psycopg2 failed to establish a TCP socket connection on port 5433 in final_version_2.py. The PostgreSQL service was either stopped or configured on default port 5432.	
A. Verified PostgreSQL service in Windows Task Manager / PowerShell.


B. Changed port from 5433 to standard 5432 in get_connection().

Before:


python\nconn = psycopg2.connect(host="localhost", port=5433, ...)\n


After:


python\nconn = psycopg2.connect(host="localhost", port=5432, ...)\n

